## Bio Note

Jan Marco Domingo is an undergraduate researcher and research assistant in the Department of Psychology at De La Salle University. As a research assistant under CORE, he primarily supports the lab’s quantitative research projects through data management and statistical analysis. His research interests include mindfulness, clinical translation, and quantitative psychology. He is also interested in topics within the psychology of religion, particularly religious fundamentalism, mindfulness across religious contexts, and intractable conflicts. Among his recent achievements is serving as an intern and researcher with the Hope Lab, where he has further developed his proficiency in advanced statistics, particularly structural equation modeling (SEM), and has been given the opportunity to contribute to a manuscript currently in preparation for publication. Outside of research, Jan enjoys learning about the food industry through documentaries and online videos, as well as exploring content on animals and the natural world, especially insects. Given the opportunity, he would gladly pursue a degree in Buddhist Studies and formal training in hypnotherapy, simply for the joy of learning.
